        <div id="womens-fit" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. How Women's Running Shoes Differ</h2>
<p>
            Most brands build their women's shoes on a separate last — the foot-shaped form a shoe
            is made around — rather than simply scaling the men's shoe down. The differences vary by
            brand and model, but three are common enough to plan around.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Heel and width.</strong> Women's lasts are usually narrower overall, and often
            narrower at the heel relative to the forefoot. Standard women's width is B, where the
            men's standard is D, so a women's wide (D) is roughly a men's standard width. That is
            why some women with wide forefeet end up in men's shoes, and why that does not always
            work: a men's shoe that fits the forefoot can leave a narrower heel slipping, which on a
            treadmill shows up as rubbing at the back of the heel on every stride.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Stack and weight.</strong> Women's versions often carry less foam and weigh less.
            The Clifton 11 is the clearest case here: reviewers give 38 mm at the heel for women
            against 42 mm for men. The Gel-Nimbus 28 is 42.5 mm for women and 43.5 mm for men. Part
            of the weight difference is just size — brands quote a women's 8 and a men's 9 or 10 — but
            not all of it. If a review you read describes a shoe as high-stack or heavy, check
            whether it was talking about the men's version.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Sizing.</strong> The commonly quoted rule is that a women's size is about one
            and a half sizes above the equivalent men's size, so a women's 9.5 is roughly a men's 8.
            Brands do not all follow it exactly, and some guides put the gap closer to a full size,
            so use the brand's own size chart if you are crossing over. Measure both feet; most
            people have one slightly longer than the other, and you size for the longer one.
          </p>
          <p>
            None of this means a women's shoe is automatically right for every woman, or a men's
            shoe wrong. It means that if a shoe almost fits, the other version, or a different width,
            may fit properly.
          </p>
        </div>

